tateMutability":"view","type":"function"}],"devdoc":{"details":"Only 32-byte roots go on-chain (no supplier data, volumes or prices).","kind":"dev","methods":{},"title":"OrbitAnchor v3: open, multi-party timestamping of supply chain ledgers","version":1},"userdoc":{"kind":"user","methods":{"anchor(bytes32,bytes32,uint64)":{"notice":"Publish a root you have checked yourself. Open to everyone."},"checkpointFor(bytes32,uint256)":{"notice":"The i-th checkpoint published for a ledger (in submission order)."},"checkpointsFor(bytes32,uint256,uint256)":{"notice":"Up to `max` checkpoints for a ledger starting at position `from`, in one call."},"operator()":{"notice":"Orbit's own anchoring wallet (the deployer). Informational: it has no special rights."},"verifyConsistency(uint256,uint256,bytes32[])":{"notice":"RFC 9162 \u00a72.1.4.2: checkpoint `newer` extends checkpoint `older` by appending only."},"verifyInclusion(uint256,bytes32,uint256,bytes32[])":{"notice":"RFC 9162 \u00a72.1.3.2: `entryHash` is leaf `index` of checkpoint `id`."}},"notice":"Anyone can publish the Merkle root of an Orbit ledger (the operator, an auditor, a         recycler, an NGO), and every checkpoint records who submitted it. Which submitters to         trust is the verifier's choice: Orbit's clients pin the operator address and named         witnesses, and treat every other submitter as unidentified.         Trees follow RFC 6962 / RFC 9162 (as in Certificate Transparency):           leaf = sha256(0x00 || entryHash), node = sha256(0x01 || left || right).         - verifyInclusion proves an entry is in a checkpoint (index- and size-bound).         - verifyConsistency proves a later checkpoint only APPENDS to an earlier one, so a           rewrite of anchored history can never be made consistent, whatever its length.","version":1}},"settings":{"compilationTarget":{"OrbitAnchor.sol":"OrbitAnchor"},"evmVersion":"paris","libraries":{},"metadata":{"bytecodeHash":"ipfs"},"optimizer":{"enabled":true,"runs":2